# Netlify edge rules. No build step — Netlify reads this file directly.
#
# The repo root IS the publish directory, so every committed file is served.
# Non-public portal source (schema.sql, the SQL test harness) therefore does NOT
# live in this repo at all — it is in Hubspot/portal_seed/db/, outside the
# publish directory.
#
# That is deliberate rather than belt-and-braces: Netlify's `*` is a trailing
# splat, not a filename glob, so a rule like `/portal/*.sql` does NOT match and
# would have served the schema while looking like it was blocked. Keeping the
# files out of the directory is the only guarantee that does not depend on
# redirect semantics.
#
# portal/ now contains only what the browser is meant to fetch: the five pages,
# portal.css and portal.js.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INTERNAL DOCUMENTS ARE NOT PUBLIC. Added 26 Aug 2026, after DECISIONS.md was
# found returning 200 on the deploy preview — 305 KB of contractor pay, brand
# margins and invoice figures, on a public URL with no login. Production was
# spared only because those files were not on `main` yet; merging the PR would
# have published every one of them.
#
# THE FILES MOVED INTO `docs/` RATHER THAN BEING BLOCKED WHERE THEY LAY, and
# that is the whole point. Netlify's `*` is a TRAILING splat, not a filename
# glob — the note at the top of this file records that `/portal/*.sql` does not
# match and would have served the schema while looking blocked (D12). A
# directory prefix is the one shape the splat really does cover.
#
# The `!` is required. Netlify serves a matching static file IN PREFERENCE to a
# redirect unless the rule is forced, so `404` without the bang would have left
# every one of these files readable while this block sat here looking like it
# had closed them.
/docs/*      /404.html   404!

# CLAUDE.md cannot move: Claude Code reads it from the project root. It carries
# the same commercial detail as the rest, so it is blocked by exact path — which
# works, unlike a filename glob, because it is a literal path and not a pattern.
/CLAUDE.md   /404.html   404!
